\subsection{The Min System: Biology Inverts the Control Direction}
\label{sec:inversion}

The most conspicuous real-world test of our claim appears, at first sight, to run against it. In extant bacteria the direction of control between spatial organization and division is the \emph{reverse} of the one our physics produces. The MinCDE system of \emph{Escherichia coli} is a self-organizing reaction--diffusion oscillator: MinD binds the membrane through an ATPase cycle, MinE drives it pole to pole, and the time-averaged MinC concentration is lowest at midcell, so FtsZ polymerizes there and nowhere else \citep{lutkenhaus2007min}. Here the spatial Map positions the division machinery. Min's control is primarily positional rather than temporal --- it selects the division site, not the division interval --- but the direction of causation, pattern to division apparatus, is unambiguous, and \emph{min} mutants that lose it divide aberrantly and produce minicells. This is Map\,$\to$\,Clock, and it is arguably the best-characterized case of pattern formation doing division-related work anywhere in the literature. A referee is entitled to ask why a paper arguing that the Clock precedes and enables the Map does not simply lose to it.

Our answer is that the two directions are successive layers rather than competitors, and that which one comes first is the substance of the claim. What our 1{,}845 runs establish is that Clock\,$\to$\,Map comes for free: membrane mechanics regularizes division with no information-bearing machinery whatever, and persistent spatial structure then consolidates inside the temporal window that regularity opens. Nothing in that chain requires a gene, a protein, or a selected function. Map\,$\to$\,Clock control is a categorically different object. It requires a spatial field shaped to report cell geometry and a division apparatus built to read it --- in the Min case three proteins, an ATPase cycle, and a membrane-binding switch, none of them available prebiotically.

The Phase-E test reported in \S5.3 asks whether that second layer nevertheless falls out of the first for free. It does not. If thermodynamic selection on organization were sufficient to install Map\,$\to$\,Clock control, runs reaching the Engine phase should show \emph{less} Map-driven Clock disruption than runs that stall at Phase C; we observe the opposite, with a bootstrap 95\% CI of $[+0.106, +0.144]$ on the difference. Selecting for organization does not, by itself, discriminate between Maps that assist the Clock and Maps that degrade it. The inversion has to be paid for separately.

This sharpens what the Genesis Engine does and does not claim. The Trinity ordering describes the assembly of the primordial substrate: Clock, then Map, then Engine, from physics alone. The Min system describes what selection later built \emph{on top of} that substrate --- a feedback loop converting the Map from a passive consequence of division timing into an active controller of division geometry. Our Phase-E null locates the boundary between the two: everything up to and including thermodynamic selection on organization is spontaneous in our model, and the Map\,$\to$\,Clock inversion is the first step that is not. That boundary is a concrete target for synthetic biology, because it names the minimum functionality that must be supplied rather than waited for. It also yields a prediction: reconstituting a Min-like positioning system inside a vesicle whose division is driven by pure geometric instability should not, on its own, improve division-interval regularity, because the vesicle has no mechanism for reading the pattern. Regularity should improve only once the division trigger is itself made pattern-sensitive --- precisely the coupling a v6 model with an explicit fitness penalty on $\Delta\mathrm{CV}$ is designed to evolve.

